{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a man from among you offers a sacrifice to YHWH from the animal</b> The full measure of this verse is: \"When a man from among you offers a sacrifice to YHWH from the animal, from the cattle or from the flock you shall offer it.\" The reason is that afterward He commands concerning the bird offering and the meal offering, so He states here: when a man offers an animal sacrifice, he shall offer it from these two species, and not a wild animal and not other beasts. Now this is a negative precept derived from an affirmative commandment for one who offers a wild animal, as they said in Zevaḥim, third chapter (34a): one who offers limbs of a wild animal — Rabbi Yoḥanan says he transgresses an affirmative commandment." "<b>You shall offer</b> — this teaches that two persons may volunteer a burnt offering in partnership. <b>Your offering</b> — this teaches that it may come as a freewill offering of the congregation; it is the burnt offering of the surplus of the altar (עוֹלַת קַיִץ הַמִּזְבֵּחַ) that comes from the leftovers — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 1:2). The Rav likewise explained that if many volunteer to bring a burnt offering, it is an offering of partners; what difference is there between two who partner in an offering and ten or a thousand who partner in it? But the surplus of the altar that comes from the leftovers — the court designates them for this purpose, and therefore it is a burnt offering of the congregation. Now according to his view, any burnt offering that many people bring other than from the leftovers has the same law as an offering of partners, and all require semikhah (leaning) and libations brought from their own means. And perhaps according to his view the bird offering, which may come as a freewill offering of two and does not come as a freewill offering of the congregation, and likewise the shelamim offerings about which they said that partners bring them as a freewill offering but the congregation does not bring them as a freewill offering (Torat Kohanim, chapter 16, section 5) — in all of these the many may volunteer for them at the outset, for it is called \"a burnt offering of partners\" and they are called \"shelamim of partners,\" and they are not excluded except that they may not bring them from the collection chests. And it is possible to say that if the congregation wished to set aside funds at the outset for a freewill offering and collect it just as they collect the shekel dues for the daily and additional offerings, it would be a freewill offering of the congregation in an animal burnt offering, and there would be no semikhah, for it is derived from this verse, and as long as the majority of Israel volunteer in it, it is a freewill offering of the congregation; and this does not apply to the bird offering or to shelamim, and a minority of them are judged as individuals — and this is the primary principle."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:32.842743961Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}